namespace pdg {
/**
\class Collider
\ingroup Physics
\brief Optional shapes, filtering, sensors and contacts for a Sprite, Part or Particle.

Use Sprite::setupCollider(), Part::setupCollider() or Particle::setupCollider(). Their read-only `collider`
property returns the same handle until removeCollider(); beforehand it returns
shared Collider::NoCollider. Collider is independent of animation, artwork and
physical motion. Creating one never creates a PhysicsBody.

Local circles, capsules, rectangles and polygons follow their owner's affine
transform, including parent/bone motion, scale and reflection. Nonuniformly
scaled or sheared circles and capsules use convex approximations; see
\ref collider_capsule_geometry. Singular geometry
does not participate in the collision world. getBounds(), contains() and
overlaps() are explicit geometric queries and ignore event filters.

Enable SpriteLayer::enableCollisions() for contacts within a layer, or
SpriteLayer::enableCollisionsWithLayer() for a linked pair of layers. Connected
PhysicsBodies suppress their mutual contacts unless each connecting constraint
allows them. Multiple shapes on the same body do not collide with one another.
Contacts between linked layers using different solvers use basic impulse response.

A solid collider uses its owner's body by default. Without a body it acts as an
externally positioned, immovable surface with no simulated velocity. Use a
kinematic PhysicsBody for a moving platform that must carry or push objects.
setPhysicsBody() explicitly contributes a Part's geometry to a compound body;
useOwnerPhysics() restores the default association. Sensors report overlaps
without impulses and work without any physical body.

\note Both Sprite and Part owners advance automatically. Native contact callbacks
and opted-in eventType_ColliderContact events run after solving. Callbacks can
remove colliders, bodies, owners or layers; contact payloads describe the solved
frame, so another callback may already have detached a referenced collider.

\code{.js}
const layer = pdg.createSpriteLayer();
layer.enableCollisions();
const sprite = layer.createSprite();
const foot = sprite.createPart('foot');
foot.setupPhysicsBody(1, 10);
foot.setupCollider().setCircle(8).setWantsContactEvents(true);
sprite.addHandler(new pdg.IEventHandler(function(event) {
    if (event.phase === pdg.collision_Begin && event.collider === foot.collider) {
        console.log('Foot contacted shape ' + event.otherShape);
    }
    return true;
}), pdg.eventType_ColliderContact);
\endcode

Retained handles remain queryable after removal, using the final transform;
callbacks and physical associations are cleared. Initial Sprite/Layer snapshots
preserve collider geometry/configuration, compound body links and constraints.
Use a Layer snapshot for links between Sprites. Callback filters and contact handlers
cannot be saved; reinstall them after loading. Native callers can also use
independent Collider and CollisionWorld objects for custom owner types.

\see PhysicsBody, PhysicsConstraint, ColliderContact
*/
/**
\var Collider::NoCollider
\brief Shared absence object for every Sprite or Part without collision geometry.

Compare by identity. Mutators safely do nothing, with a debug diagnostic; they
do not accumulate settings for later creation. Its geometry is empty.
*/
/** \var Sprite::collider
\brief Read-only Collider association, or shared Collider::NoCollider.
\see Sprite::setupCollider(), Sprite::removeCollider()
*/
/** \var Part::collider
\brief Read-only Collider association, or shared Collider::NoCollider.
\see Part::setupCollider(), Part::removeCollider()
*/
}
